---
/**
 * The topic list — "Topics: systems thinking, mental models" or a stacked list,
 * depending on how much width it is given.
 *
 * ── IT INFERS ITS LAYOUT FROM ITS OWN WIDTH, NOT FROM THE PAGE ───────────────
 *
 * Two consumers wrote these rules independently — the article page's rail and the
 * note entry — which is DESIGN.md's trigger for promotion. Extracting them raised the
 * real question: the note is an inline run at EVERY width, the article rail is an
 * inline run below md and a stacked list from md, and a `layout` prop is the obvious
 * way to say so.
 *
 * It is the wrong way, because the page is not what decides. What decides is whether
 * this list is sitting in the RAIL COLUMN or in the MAIN COLUMN — a question about
 * the space it occupies, which is exactly what a size container query answers and
 * what DESIGN.md nominates:
 *
 *   "Prefer a real size container query for those, which answers to the space the
 *    component occupies rather than to the viewport, so the same card in the rail
 *    and at full width get different treatment from one rule."
 *
 * So neither caller configures anything, and a third — a `presentation` rail, a
 * services page — is correct without being told. The article page's md breakpoint
 * for this disappears: it was never about the viewport, it was about the list moving
 * columns AT that viewport.
 *
 * ── THE 16rem THRESHOLD, AND WHY IT CLEARS BOTH SIDES ────────────────────────
 *
 *   rail        150 at md (768) -> 231 from ~1028 up, capped by the 996 grid
 *   main column 288 at a 320 viewport -> 343 at 375 -> 440 at md -> 656 at 1440
 *
 * 16rem is 256: about 25px above the rail's widest and 32px below the main column's
 * narrowest. 18rem would sit more centrally between 231 and 343 and would flip a
 * 320px phone to the stacked form, which is why it is not used — 343 is the
 * comfortable floor, not the real one.
 *
 * ── THE INNER ELEMENT IS NOT GRATUITOUS ──────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * A container query never restyles its own container, so the element declaring
 * `container-type` cannot be the one that switches between flex and block. The <nav>
 * measures; `.topic-list-inner` responds. ArticleCard hit the same wall and its
 * header records the same fix.
 */
import {slugify} from '../lib/slug'

interface Props {
  /** Already narrowed to non-null by the caller. */
  topics: string[]
  /**
   * The heading's id, which `aria-labelledby` also points at. The CALLER supplies it
   * because it has to be namespaced against that page's own body heading ids, which
   * are slugified from heading text — an h2 titled "Topics" would otherwise collide.
   */
  id: string
  /** Outline level only. The component pins the type; see the note on the label. */
  as?: 'h2' | 'h3'
  /** Emit mf2 `p-category`. Only inside an `h-entry` — Eyebrow's `microformats`
      opt-in, and for the same reason: a category outside any entry root is noise a
      parser has to ignore. */
  microformats?: boolean
  class?: string
  /* Forwarded to the root element, which is what lets a PAGE place this component:
     Astro passes the parent's scope down as a prop and it reaches the DOM only if the
     component spreads rest. PLACEMENT IS THE CALLER'S — this file sets no
     `grid-column`, the same boundary the cards draw around their outdent. */
  [key: string]: unknown
}

const {
  topics,
  id,
  as: Heading = 'h3',
  microformats = false,
  class: className,
  ...rest
}: Props = Astro.props
---

<nav class:list={['topic-list', className]} aria-labelledby={id} {...rest}>
  <div class="topic-list-inner">
    <Heading id={id} class="topic-list-label">Topics</Heading>
    <ul role="list">
      {
        topics.map((topic) => (
          <li>
            {/*
              PROVISIONAL HREF. docs/urls-and-filtering.md retires `/insights/tag/{slug}/`
              in favour of a filter param, and its own redirect rule —
              `^/insights/tag/(.+?)/?$ -> /insights?topic=$1` — is what fixes the VALUE
              shape as the slugified prefLabel. So the transform is right and the
              plumbing is not: the param should ultimately come from the facet index's
              resolvable slugs per concept, resolved in the query.

              `p-category` and NOT `u-category`, which is the easy one to get wrong:
              `p-` takes the element's TEXT, so this publishes the topic's label, which
              is what a reader or a syndicated copy wants as a tag. `u-` would take the
              href and publish the provisional filter URL above — a link nobody outside
              this site can use, and one that is going to change.
            */}
            <a
              class:list={[microformats && 'p-category']}
              href={`/insights/?topic=${slugify(topic)}`}
            >
              {topic}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </div>
</nav>

<style>
  .topic-list {
    /* What makes the layout query possible. Also contains the inline axis, which is
       what we want: this takes its width from the column it is placed in, never from
       its own contents. */
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  /* ── Wide: the inline run, and it is the BASE case ───────────────────────── */
  /*
   * "Topics:  systems thinking,  mental models" — one wrapping run where row two
   * starts at the column's left edge UNDER the label rather than indented past it.
   * That is why the label and every item have to be siblings in ONE flex container:
   * `display: contents` on the <ul> lifts the <li>s up beside the heading. A nested
   * flex container would wrap inside its own box.
   *
   * `gap: var(--space-1)` gives the boards' 8 in both axes. Rows land 34 apart — the
   * 26px line box plus 8 — which inline layout could not produce, since its row
   * spacing is line-height and nothing else.
   *
   * ── WHY THIS IS THE BASE AND THE STACKED FORM IS THE QUERY ─────────────────
   *
   * tokens.css's rule is that an engine which never evaluates the query must be left
   * with the phone layout. On a phone BOTH consumers show the inline run — the rail
   * has dispersed into the main column by then — so the run is the honest base. The
   * cost of no container-query support is a wide-form list in a 231px rail: cramped,
   * not broken.
   */
  .topic-list-inner {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-1);
  }

  /* `display: contents` lifts the <li>s into the flex row above. base.css keeps the
     UA's list indent for prose; a nav is not prose. */
  .topic-list ul {
    display: contents;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /*
   * ── THE COMPONENT OWNS ITS TYPE; `as` OWNS ONLY THE OUTLINE LEVEL ──────────
   *
   * The cards' rule, and it earns its place here twice over: the note passes `as="h2"`
   * and the article rail `as="h3"`, and base.css gives those two different sizes AND
   * different tracking. Without pinning all three properties the same list would draw
   * at 28.125/35.16 with -1.5% tracking on one page and 22.5/29.25 on the other.
   *
   * `letter-spacing: normal` is the one that hides: h3 carries none, so the article
   * page never needed it and the note did. Stated unconditionally so the level a
   * caller picks stays a document-outline decision and costs nothing visually.
   */
  .topic-list-label {
    font-size: var(--font-size-2);
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: normal;
  }

  .topic-list-label::after {
    content: ':';
  }

  /* Inside the <li>, after the <a> — so the comma is not part of the link's text and
     does not take the hover underline with it. */
  .topic-list li:not(:last-child)::after {
    content: ',';
  }

  /* ── Narrow: the stacked list, when this sits in a rail ──────────────────── */
  @container (inline-size < 16rem) {
    .topic-list-inner,
    .topic-list ul {
      display: block;
    }

    /* The h3 role. Still whatever element `as` chose — the level is the outline's
       business and only the presentation changes here. */
    .topic-list-label {
      font-size: var(--font-size-3);
      line-height: 1.3;
    }

    .topic-list-label::after,
    .topic-list li:not(:last-child)::after {
      content: none;
    }

    /* The rhythm ramp from base.css restated for a context it does not reach: 16 from
       a heading to its list, and `rhythm-item` between items.

       THE ITEM GAP IS ASKED FOR HERE, not inherited. base.css carried a global
       `li + li` margin until 2026-09-02, when it was removed because eight places
       cancelled it and none wanted it; this stacked rail list is the one that did, so
       it now says so locally. See base.css → Prose. */
    .topic-list-label + ul {
      margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
    }

    .topic-list li + li {
      margin-top: var(--rhythm-item);
    }
  }

  /* ── Links ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * OPTS OUT OF THE WIPE-IN UNDERLINE, which base.css's `nav` role provides for with
   * exactly this escape hatch. The wipe is a chrome gesture for the masthead and the
   * footer; a dense run of topic links animating one by one under a moving pointer is
   * noise rather than affordance. DESIGN.md → The rail.
   *
   * THE THICKNESS HAS TO BE RESTATED, and that is easy to miss. base.css gives every
   * anchor `text-decoration-thickness: 0.06em`, then `nav :is(a, button)` resets it
   * with the `text-decoration` SHORTHAND, which includes `-thickness`. Without the
   * declaration below the underline returns at the UA's `auto` weight, and two
   * underlines in one viewport read as two systems. `text-underline-offset` is NOT in
   * the shorthand and survives on its own.
   *
   * THE LINE IS DECLARED AT REST AND ONLY ITS COLOUR MOVES, because
   * `text-decoration-line` is a discrete keyword and cannot be animated from `none` —
   * it appeared instantly while everything else on the page eased.
   *
   * These rules are now in two places, not one: the article page's `.rail a` still
   * covers its "On This Page" links, which this component cannot reach. That is a
   * genuine remainder rather than an oversight — if a THIRD dense link list appears,
   * the answer is to promote the treatment, not to write it a third time.
   *
   * The hover COLOUR is left to base.css's global `a:hover`. This sits on the page
   * ground, where `link-hover` is right and the underline is not the only signal.
   */
  .topic-list a::after {
    content: none;
  }

  .topic-list a {
    text-decoration-line: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 0.06em;
    text-decoration-color: transparent;
    transition:
      color 0.15s,
      text-decoration-color 0.15s;
  }

  .topic-list a:hover {
    text-decoration-color: currentColor;
  }
</style>
